{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1379141-how-to-fit-primers-and-mage-training-programs-into-the-game","id":1379141,"title":"How to fit Primers and Mage training programs into the game?","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","World of Darkness","Mage: The Ascension"],"posts":[{"date":"2020-04-06T19:41:52-07:00","id":1379141,"title":"How to fit Primers and Mage training programs into the game?","content":"I personally like the idea of Grimoires and Primers, but am not sure how to fit them into the game. My personal fan idea of a Primer involves a huge tome made from the skin and bones of a complete pack (all Auspices) of werewolves (they were dicks who tried to wipe out the Chantry for its Node). The book generates its own Delerium effect as though it were a Crinos, and on top of having to have Intelligence 3 and having to know the mortal, hedge magic version of Enochian, you also have to have a Willpower of 10, to allow the Delerium to wash over you, and through you, and guide your mind and spirit to enlightenment, rather than just break you both physically and spiritually. It is in fact going to be a center piece for a story I'm writing up for a group I'm putting together, and I wanted thoughts and insights on how I could employ it. <br /> <br /> My question, is can Primers really exist in the setting when it is supposed to be so hard to Awaken and Mages are supposed to be so very rare? I personally don't mind making mages slightly more common, and I really like the idea of tools that help you achieve that final push into Enlightenment. Perhaps I should just ignore a few aspects of the game to help the story go forward?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/986-mercurial","name":"Mercurial","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar986_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1297"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-04-06T21:12:30-07:00","id":1379171,"title":"","content":"Of course they can exist; the Sons of Ether were founded on one. The catch is that reading the Primer doesn't guarantee that you'll Awaken; a lot of other factors need to align. Think of the Primer more as a catalyst: there must already be something driving the potential mage toward Awakening, and in a manner that suitable to the nature of the Primer; but it can't be so strong of an impetus that she Awakens on her own. Reading the Primer weakens the barrier that's preventing her from Awakening enough to allow her to Awaken. <br /> <br /> A Primer that's read by someone who isn't ready to Awaken, or whose Focus isn't going to be compatible with the teachings of the Primer, won't do anything for that reader.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div style=\"margin-left:40px\"> <div align=\"center\">My hacks: <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwtrin\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#0000FF\">Trinity Continuum</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwscion\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#008000\">Scion</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwExalt\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#DAA520\">Exalted</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwcwod\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#800000\">World of Darkness</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwnwod\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#FF0000\">Chronicles of Darkness</span></a></div> </div>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/421-dataweaver","name":"Dataweaver","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar421_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"8420"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-04-06T22:58:49-07:00","id":1379181,"title":"","content":"Also, as presented in Ascension's Landscape on the Storyteller's Vault and even work's such as Paul Strack's Mage Demographics back from 1990s discussions of Mage, there isn't a set number of mages in the world. Each group should chose how frequently Awakening occurs, the survival rate of mages to various power levels (as in, do most mages that make it out of the Apprentice stage end up becoming a Master?) and other questions.<br /> <br /> The OP's proposed primer is considerably narrower in usability than any of the primers and grimoires in Forged in Dragon's Fire and other Mage books from Mage: the Ascension (I'm not that up on Sorcerer's Crusade or Dark Ages) except the Uranian Manual. Even that doesn't require Willpower 10 to use.<br /> <br /> Mage being a game about reshaping reality beckons to each group to make minor tweaks of things they don't like to be more fun. If you are discussing things with others, just be kind and let them know when departing from canon.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/13680-baakyocalder","name":"baakyocalder","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar13680_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"353"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-04-07T06:48:27-07:00","id":1379246,"title":"","content":"Your Primer makes it already quiet hard for anyone to read it^^<br /> <br /> But even wit a normal with a more mundane Primer, it's not anyone can take it and read it.First you have to have access to on. Second you have to read it back to back. Third you have to really understand what is written there on a fundamental level. Fourth, you have then also believe it to be true<br /> <br /> So imagine something like Heidegger's \u201cBeing and Time\u201d as a Primer. Now access is fairly easy. But now that you have it you have to read the dam thing. Cover to cover. Oh and now you have to actually understand what the guy is saying there. Not just vaguely or kind of maybe. No UNDERSTAND it. And then the last issue, yes you read it, yes you even really understood what he was trying to say there, BUT that doesn't mean you believe in it. You could still say\u201d Yeah well, some interesting points and all, but I don't believe this is how things actually work.\u201d<br /> <br /> Also as mentioned above, the number of Mages largely depends on the chronicle/book your currently reading. And OoH, Verbena etc. seem to be able to largely train people with a certain success rate into Awakening.<br /> <br /> Also, even in a setting where the number of reasonably magickally competent tradition mages is rather low, there could still be a fair amount of people who in game terms have Avatar 0, Arete 1, and 1 or 2 Spheres at 1, who will never rise to more. They experience the world a little truer/deeper, but that's about it.<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/25507-nobby","name":"nobby","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar25507_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2019"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"66"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-04-08T00:08:01-07:00","id":1379420,"title":"","content":"Given that the point of a Primer is to Awaken people and spread the creator's Paradigm, I don't think anyone would make one as difficult to engage with as the example the OP described.<br /> <br /> Except Hermetic House Tytalus, I suppose...<br /> <br /> <br /> More to the point, Dataweaver is more or less correct. A Primer alone won't drag a person into Awakening. The person must be able and willing to meet it halfway.<br /> <br /> First, they must have the reading comprehension to grok the lessons the text is trying to impart. Reader must be able to understand the material, rather than it washing off like water off a duck's back. One imagines a Primer is complex enough that you really need to contemplate it in order to absorb the implications.<br /> <br /> Second, the reader mustn't be resistant to accepting the lesson as true. The biggest obstacle in the way of a person's Awakening is their own attachment to the world as they understand it. Awakening requires - and I will never apologize for the pun - a paradigm shift. Present all the evidence you like, the reader has to choose to believe it. The Primer will take a person to the edge; the reader must decide to make the leap.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div align=\"center\"><a href=\"http://archiveofourown.org/works/6956863/chapters/15862327\" target=\"_blank\">Magestuck: The Ascension</a></div>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/6156-bluecho","name":"Bluecho","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar6156_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3871"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-04-08T11:54:48-07:00","id":1379509,"title":"","content":"From what I recall, Primers really only work if not only they're open to the lessons contained within, but if they actively try to implement them in their lives.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11744-enginseer-42","name":"Enginseer-42","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"774"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-04-08T12:16:25-07:00","id":1379510,"title":"","content":"There's a biblical adage that \u201cfaith without works is dead\u201d. If you profess a belief in something, but it doesn't affect the way you live your life, do you really believe it? <br /> <br /> So yeah: reading a Primer and nodding your head and saying \u201cyeah, that makes sense\u201d and doing nothing else isn't going to lead to an Awakening. To Awaken, you must attempt to put the lessons in the Primer into practice. <br /> <br /> The point is that the use of a Primer is something intensely personal; you're not going to be able to use one to mass-produce mages. Conversely, it's not without value; it <i>does</i> make it more likely that the reader will Awaken \u2014 and arguably, that's not just because the arguments in its pages are persuasive. It is, after all, a Wonder.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div style=\"margin-left:40px\"> <div align=\"center\">My hacks: <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwtrin\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#0000FF\">Trinity Continuum</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwscion\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#008000\">Scion</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwExalt\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#DAA520\">Exalted</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwcwod\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#800000\">World of Darkness</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwnwod\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#FF0000\">Chronicles of Darkness</span></a></div> </div>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/421-dataweaver","name":"Dataweaver","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar421_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"8420"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-04-08T15:22:01-07:00","id":1379528,"title":"","content":"<br /> Ultimately, I think it depends on what you want to do with the game. If you just want to have them be a regular, comparatively &quot;normal&quot; thing, then it would, I think, just be a simple matter of saying &quot;these things exist, and are even the norm for Awakenings, but the success rate is such that the number of Awakened is the same as normally presented in the game setting&quot;. On the other hand, the sort of book you describe could be some sort of ultra-rare/unique artifact that is viewed as a potential game changer in the Ascension War with everyone desperately trying to take possession of it. Either works perfectly fine. <br /> <br /> I will mention, however, that in terms of Willpower, the average &quot;Joe Normal&quot; person has a WP of 2 or maybe 3. People with a WP of 10 are even rarer than Awakened.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><i>What is tolerance? It is the consequence of humanity. We are all formed of frailty and error; let us pardon reciprocally each other's folly. That is the first law of nature.</i><br /> Voltaire, &quot;Tolerance&quot; (1764)","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12890-no-one-of-consequence","name":"No One of Consequence","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12890_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3937"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-04-08T17:07:41-07:00","id":1379553,"title":"","content":"A starting Mage has 5 Willpower. 10 Willpower is 5 freebie points, or one-third of the freebie point build.<br /> <br /> Drop the Willpower required and the primer is still idiosyncratic, but could actually work for more than a few people.<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/13680-baakyocalder","name":"baakyocalder","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar13680_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"353"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-04-10T19:57:48-07:00","id":1380283,"title":"","content":"As I read <i>Forged By Dragon's Fire</i>, a Primer awakens someone <i>if</i> they're open to the magical teachings within (so you can't be reading the thing merely as a scholarly pursuit thinking it's all hogwash and accidentally awaken), you affirmatively incorporate and perform the teachings contained within, <i>and</i> have an Intelligence Trait of 4 or higher. If all those things are true, X number of weeks later (the book says how many--it's like between 1 and 3 months), the person awakens. No roll as I recall--it just happens. NOW, good luck findings someone with <b>(a)</b> that level of Intelligence (statistically rare),<b> (b) </b>who is genuinely open to potentially radical and heterodox ideas (i.e., the substantive content of the Primer), and <b>(c) </b>who is going to buy in enough to actually 'get with the program' and do what the Primer says.<br /> <br /> Mind you, I think a shortcut to getting people to take it seriously is to do some wild crap in front of them so they'll take it seriously, but it's still not gonna be easy-peasy.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Sig","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2319-captotter","name":"CaptOtter","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2319_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"405"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-04-13T08:32:18-07:00","id":1380967,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>CaptOtter</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1379141-how-to-fit-primers-and-mage-training-programs-into-the-game?p=1380283#post1380283\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">As I read <i>Forged By Dragon's Fire</i>, a Primer awakens someone <i>if</i> they're open to the magical teachings within (so you can't be reading the thing merely as a scholarly pursuit thinking it's all hogwash and accidentally awaken), you affirmatively incorporate and perform the teachings contained within, <i>and</i> have an Intelligence Trait of 4 or higher. If all those things are true, X number of weeks later (the book says how many--it's like between 1 and 3 months), the person awakens. No roll as I recall--it just happens. NOW, good luck findings someone with <b>(a)</b> that level of Intelligence (statistically rare),<b> (b) </b>who is genuinely open to potentially radical and heterodox ideas (i.e., the substantive content of the Primer), and <b>(c) </b>who is going to buy in enough to actually 'get with the program' and do what the Primer says.<br /> <br /> Mind you, I think a shortcut to getting people to take it seriously is to do some wild crap in front of them so they'll take it seriously, but it's still not gonna be easy-peasy.</div> </div> </div> </div>Personally, looking back on it, I kind of dislike Primers having an Intelligence minimum. Or at least having a minimum of 4. It runs contrary to the spirit of Mage, where you don't need to be stereotypical erudite wizard in order to do magic. Not all Primers, according to the Paradigm that made it, should require the person be one step removed for genius-level intellect.<br /> <br /> But that's just me.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div align=\"center\"><a href=\"http://archiveofourown.org/works/6956863/chapters/15862327\" target=\"_blank\">Magestuck: The Ascension</a></div>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/6156-bluecho","name":"Bluecho","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar6156_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3871"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-04-13T20:24:07-07:00","id":1381199,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>nobby</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1379141-how-to-fit-primers-and-mage-training-programs-into-the-game?p=1379246#post1379246\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Also, even in a setting where the number of reasonably magickally competent tradition mages is rather low, there could still be a fair amount of people who in game terms have Avatar 0, Arete 1, and 1 or 2 Spheres at 1, who will never rise to more. They experience the world a little truer/deeper, but that's about it.</div> </div> </div> </div>This is a really good point and one I never considered. Maybe there are relatively few Hermetics of the 6+ degree, but quite a few peons of the third and fourth degrees. Those who are barely awake, but still looked up to in awe by <br /> the hedge wizards, even if they spend most of their time studying and dusting shelves.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/986-mercurial","name":"Mercurial","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar986_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1297"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-04-14T19:12:49-07:00","id":1381504,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Bluecho</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1379141-how-to-fit-primers-and-mage-training-programs-into-the-game?p=1380967#post1380967\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Personally, looking back on it, I kind of dislike Primers having an Intelligence minimum. Or at least having a minimum of 4. It runs contrary to the spirit of Mage, where you don't need to be stereotypical erudite wizard in order to do magic. Not all Primers, according to the Paradigm that made it, should require the person be one step removed for genius-level intellect.<br /> <br /> But that's just me.</div> </div> </div> </div> My view on it is that while you certainly don't need any given level of intelligence to awaken (though it probably helps to have more than 1), it makes sense to me that the guaranteed, no rolls, no risk, no <i>cost</i> awakening that occurs as a result of studying a Primer (which appears more often than not to be some wieghty book) would be gated behind some kind of state--that it requires a very intelligent person, specifically, speaks to the fact that we're talking about transformative ideas beyond the ken of regular human understanding that have been reduced to a writing.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Sig","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2319-captotter","name":"CaptOtter","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2319_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"405"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-04-15T05:43:06-07:00","id":1381617,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>CaptOtter</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1379141-how-to-fit-primers-and-mage-training-programs-into-the-game?p=1381504#post1381504\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">that it requires a very intelligent person, specifically, speaks to the fact that we're talking about transformative ideas beyond the ken of regular human understanding that have been reduced to a writing.</div> </div> </div> </div>However, some Premiers aren't coherent with such trait as described in WoD.<br /> <br /> Like, say, the Premier that's described as a scroll of martial arts seems to imply that you need to do the forms perfectly in order to Awaken. Muscle memory and body intelligence seems to have more to do with that than being a good intelectual... and, really, WoD Intelligence isn't good to represent &quot;non conventional&quot; forms of Int, like the one involved in learning MA. WoD Int seems to be the intelectual kind of Int, that's not really that important for many Paradigms.<br /> <br /> The restriction, if any, should be Paradigmatical. I can see Hermetic grimoires needing Int. Others could require other Traits, like Dex or Skills like Emphaty or Science...<br /> <br /> Probably having a skillset as a gate would represent better these limitations than Atributes. I can't see someone understanding a typical Hermetic Grimoire regardless of their Int 5, if they don't have also some Knowledge to understand the <i>simbology </i>used. Science would make sense for a Premier that's trying to teach you the secrets of basic enligthened science. Skills tend to be more &quot;paradigmatical&quot; than Atributes. Of course, there's still a relation with Atributes: Like, how did you got Science 4 with Int 1? - you<i> technically can do that</i>, but most likely you have some int.<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1683-aleph","name":"Aleph","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1683_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3056"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-04-15T08:29:28-07:00","id":1381652,"title":"","content":"This. <br /> <br /> As I see it, in M20 terms a Primer has a Focus: it pushes you to Awaken by revealing a Paradigm and a Practice which you must embrace if the Primer is going to be of any benefit in your Awakening. And Practices have Associated Abilities which arguably every practitioner should know. There isn't a hardwired mandate in the books to this effect; but if you have Martial Arts as your Practice and you don't have any dots in a Martial Arts Ability, you're doing something wrong. This holds for Primers, too: you really ought to have some dots in the Associated Abilities of the Practice that the Primer is trying to teach you. <i>That</i>, rather than a mandatory four dots in Intelligence, feels like a more appropriate prerequisite to Awakening through a Primer.<br /> <br /> Also: while they don't show up in every Practice, look for Academics, Belief Systems, Esoterica, and Science as very common Associated Abilities that tend to represent knowledge of the underlying theoretical foundation of a given Practice. In fact, going through the Practices described in the core book, the only one I could find that didn't have at least one of these particular Abilities listed was Gutter Magick. If I had to pin it down to just two Abilities, it would be Esoterica (for mystical mages) and Science (for technomancers).","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div style=\"margin-left:40px\"> <div align=\"center\">My hacks: <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwtrin\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#0000FF\">Trinity Continuum</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwscion\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#008000\">Scion</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwExalt\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#DAA520\">Exalted</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwcwod\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#800000\">World of Darkness</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwnwod\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#FF0000\">Chronicles of Darkness</span></a></div> </div>","edited":{"userid":421,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/421-dataweaver","user":"Dataweaver","date":". 04-15-2020, 12:13 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/421-dataweaver","name":"Dataweaver","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar421_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"8420"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-04-16T09:53:17-07:00","id":1381938,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Aleph</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1379141-how-to-fit-primers-and-mage-training-programs-into-the-game?p=1381617#post1381617\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">However, some Premiers aren't coherent with such trait as described in WoD.<br /> <br /> Like, say, the Premier that's described as a scroll of martial arts seems to imply that you need to do the forms perfectly in order to Awaken. Muscle memory and body intelligence seems to have more to do with that than being a good intelectual... and, really, WoD Intelligence isn't good to represent &quot;non conventional&quot; forms of Int, like the one involved in learning MA. WoD Int seems to be the intelectual kind of Int, that's not really that important for many Paradigms.<br /> <br /> The restriction, if any, should be Paradigmatical. I can see Hermetic grimoires needing Int. Others could require other Traits, like Dex or Skills like Emphaty or Science...<br /> <br /> Probably having a skillset as a gate would represent better these limitations than Atributes. I can't see someone understanding a typical Hermetic Grimoire regardless of their Int 5, if they don't have also some Knowledge to understand the <i>simbology </i>used. Science would make sense for a Premier that's trying to teach you the secrets of basic enligthened science. Skills tend to be more &quot;paradigmatical&quot; than Atributes. Of course, there's still a relation with Atributes: Like, how did you got Science 4 with Int 1? - you<i> technically can do that</i>, but most likely you have some int.</div> </div> </div> </div>I'm going to argue here that a certain degree of Intelligence is nevertheless indispensable in all cases, even though I would agree that there are some Primers that should probably require <i>more </i>than simply Intelligence, if only by dint of the fact that properly executing the practices set forth in the some Primers probably require some basic understanding or capacity with another skill or knowledge.<br /> <br /> For a martial arts scroll that can confer enlightenment, I would agree than an inability to actually perform the katas (or whatever) within the scroll would be dispositive of fulfilling one of the basic requirements of benefiting from the Primer: <i>affirmative incorporation and performance of the teachings contained within </i>(to paraphrase my earlier post above). I won't belabor this because I don't think I'm disagreeing with you on this point. And while I think that's a valid gate, I think the &quot;minimum Intelligence 4&quot; -- or really, <i>some</i> minimum Intelligence (because I don't want to get caught up on the number necessarily, so much as I'm interested in discussing why a relatively above-average intelligence is necessary in <u><i>all</i></u> cases) -- is also perfectly valid.<br /> <br /> Being &quot;Awake&quot; is matter of knowledge and understanding--not &quot;Knowledge&quot; as contemplated by the character sheet (i.e., a bunch of facts about a particular subject that you have at your fingertips)--but more broadly <i>knowing; </i>you <i>know </i>and <i>understand/comprehend</i> something about the fundamental nature, and functional operation, of reality far beyond that of other mortal people. While the word &quot;Arete&quot; is subject to various interpretations (it's most basic and direct translation is &quot;excellence&quot;), it is broadly understood to be directly tied to &quot;knowledge&quot;, and is often used by classical philosophers as being interchangeable with &quot;knowledge&quot;. Wikipedia has a good article on the subject, and it's pretty well cited (just to show my sources). In furtherance of this interpretation of &quot;Arete&quot; (as used by the game), we see that the Technocracy refers to the same stat as &quot;Enlightenment&quot; (which I think is less controversially tied to knowing/understanding). As additional support, I'll point out that when the game was <i>remixed</i> into <i>Mage: The Awakening</i>, Arete became &quot;Gnosis&quot;, which does literally translate to &quot;knowledge&quot;.<br /> <br /> In my view, even if a martial arts scroll requires that you have some other attributes or skills to execute the lessons contained in a Primer, those practices are ultimately just vectors for giving rise to a state of active comprehending or knowing. The marital arts scroll may require that you go through certain movements, and build some technique, but there has to be something else in that scroll that explains how those movements or techniques relate more broadly to some theory of the cosmos/the truth of reality/etc, or some koan or puzzle. Otherwise, you'd have to believe that simply performing these particular movements a number of ways, a given number of times, would on its own confer Awakening--in which case, you wouldn't even need the scroll... someone could use Mind and Life to transfer the muscle memories and whatever else necessary to just go through the movements X number of times, and boom, you're awake--but it just doesn't make sense that you do whatever kata perefectly X number of times purely by rote mimicry, and all of a sudden, you have a new theory of reality.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Sig","edited":{"userid":2319,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2319-captotter","user":"CaptOtter","date":". 04-16-2020, 12:58 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2319-captotter","name":"CaptOtter","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2319_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"405"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-04-16T10:52:21-07:00","id":1381951,"title":"","content":"I'll have to disagree with this. If we're going to be pulling in other gamelines to talk about what Arete represents, I think that Dark Ages: Mage deserves a hearing as well. It's far more relevant than a separate gameline that, while inspired by Mage: the Ascension, is its own thing. By contrast, Dark Ages: Mage is actually part of the same world. <br /> <br /> In it, you've got the Foundation and Pillars system, where each style of magic has its own Foundation (the system's counterpoint to Arete) and Pillars (analogous to Spheres). I'll focus on the Foundations here, as that's most relevant to our current discussion. To the Ahl-i-Batin, the Foundation is Al-Ikhlas, or awareness of unity. To the Messianic Voices, the Foundation is Divinity, the relationship to the divine. To the Old Faith, the Foundation is Spontaneity, sympathy with life's pulse. To the Order of Hermes, it is Modus, formidable discipline. To the Spirit-Talkers, it is Sensitivity, the call of the Invisible World. And to the Valdaermen, it is Bl\u00f4t, the depth of sacrifice. <br /> <br /> What Arete is, beyond the generic \u201cthe innate quality that lets you perform magic\u201d, is intentionally <i>not</i> pinned down. It's left as an aspect of a mage's Focus. Since the Union strives for a more or less uniform approach to what they do, it's not surprising that they have a more specific definition for their analog of Arete. But in the big picture, Enlightenment isn't a different name for Arete; it is the Union's Foundation. It is but one take on what Arete could be. <br /> <br /> My premise here is that a Foundation is analogous to a take on what Arete might be. And different Foundations place importance on different traits, or sometimes none at all. While Enlightenment would seem to favor Intelligence, Spontaneity feels more like a Wits thing, and Sensitivity seems to be more about Perception. And I have no idea what Attribute Divinity would favor; the personal qualities that it places value on are ones that simply don't appear on a standard Mage character sheet. Essentially, it would be something closer to what Vampire calls Conscience. Modus is more of a Self-Control thing, while Bl\u00f4t could be seen to favor a kind of Courage, a willingness to suffer in order to get what you're after. <br /> <br /> All of these are valid takes in what Arete could be, and none of them should be set up on a pedestal as being more essential than the others.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div style=\"margin-left:40px\"> <div align=\"center\">My hacks: <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwtrin\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#0000FF\">Trinity Continuum</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwscion\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#008000\">Scion</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwExalt\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#DAA520\">Exalted</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwcwod\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#800000\">World of Darkness</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwnwod\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#FF0000\">Chronicles of Darkness</span></a></div> </div>","edited":{"userid":421,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/421-dataweaver","user":"Dataweaver","date":". 04-16-2020, 02:48 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/421-dataweaver","name":"Dataweaver","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar421_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"8420"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-04-16T14:02:14-07:00","id":1381974,"title":"","content":"Also calling officially, the most important characteristic of a mage isn't understanding. It's drive. There's a reason why majors automatically start with a willpower rating of 5, and why Willpower caps Arete. Where Primers are concerned, they aren't really dispensers of knowledge. I mean, they do that; but that's not what makes them Primers. What makes them Primers is that they serve as a call to action to the mage's Avatar, challenging it to Awaken. That's why it's not enough to just read what the Primer has to say; you need to <i>do</i> something about it.<br /> <br /> I have a <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1190573-redefining-mystics-and-technomancers\">house rule</a> that lets you incorporate something more along the lines of <b>Awakening</b>'s Gnosis, where Magick isn't necessarily so much about employing an innate quality of the mage to bend reality to his will, but can instead be about uncovering hidden secrets about reality. But three things about it: first, I fully and readily acknowledge that it's a house rule. Second, I don't <i>replace</i> Arete-style magick with Gnosis-style magick; I suggest the latter as an <i>alternative to</i> the former, for those character concepts that make more sense in terms of the mage tapping into innate magick in the world around him rather than being innately magickal himself. And third, even then it's not necessarily a matter of cerebral understanding: depending on the nature of the mage, it may be a more visceral thing; or if the idea is that the magick in the world is somehow alive and has a will of its own, the kind of understanding needed might be more of an empathetic bond that would be better represented by social traits than mental ones. <br /> <br /> Bottom line: even if you end up deciding to make understanding more important than motivation, Intelligence isn't necessarily the best way to represent the kinds of understanding needed.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div style=\"margin-left:40px\"> <div align=\"center\">My hacks: <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwtrin\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#0000FF\">Trinity Continuum</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwscion\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#008000\">Scion</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwExalt\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#DAA520\">Exalted</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwcwod\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#800000\">World of Darkness</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwnwod\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#FF0000\">Chronicles of Darkness</span></a></div> </div>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/421-dataweaver","name":"Dataweaver","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar421_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"8420"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-04-16T16:14:09-07:00","id":1382005,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Dataweaver</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1379141-how-to-fit-primers-and-mage-training-programs-into-the-game?p=1381951#post1381951\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I'll have to disagree with this. If we're going to be pulling in other gamelines to talk about what Arete represents, I think that Dark Ages: Mage deserves a hearing as well. It's far more relevant than a separate gameline that, while inspired by Mage: the Ascension, is its own thing. By contrast, Dark Ages: Mage is actually part of the same world.<br /> <br /> In it, you've got the Foundation and Pillars system, where each style of magic has its own Foundation (the system's counterpoint to Arete) and Pillars (analogous to Spheres). I'll focus on the Foundations here, as that's most relevant to our current discussion. To the Ahl-i-Batin, the Foundation is Al-Ikhlas, or awareness of unity. To the Messianic Voices, the Foundation is Divinity, the relationship to the divine. To the Old Faith, the Foundation is Spontaneity, sympathy with life's pulse. To the Order of Hermes, it is Modus, formidable discipline. To the Spirit-Talkers, it is Sensitivity, the call of the Invisible World. And to the Valdaermen, it is Bl\u00f4t, the depth of sacrifice.</div> </div> </div> </div>My argument here would be that <i>all </i>of the Foundations and Pillars were ultimately able to be distilled down into a singular model (i.e., Arete and the Spheres)--and while the Hermetics may have ultimately had more say in what that model was (and thus why its a Greek word that featured prominently in classical Western philosophy, as opposed to a word from Sanskrit, Yoruba, Navajo, or Proto-Indo-European), it clearly functioned well enough that everyone was able to hold on to their own paradigms, theories, styles, and trappings in the transition. In other words, Arete appears to function well enough as a common denominator for Al-Ikhlas, Divinity, Spontaneity, etc. If we're going to take as highly instructive the meaning of the individual words used to name what each tradition was calling that component of the magic system before it was called &quot;Arete&quot;, I think you have to also give due weight and consideration to the meaning and functional historical usage of the word &quot;Arete&quot;.<br /> <br /> With regard to how instructive <i>Mage: Awakening </i>is, I agree that it's not as persuasive as Dark Ages: Mage, when I addressed that above. Having said that, I will note that the person who wrote the section of <i>Forged by Dragon's Fire</i> dealing with Grimoires (and therefore Primers), Kraig Blackwelder, is also listed as a contributor to the revised Core Book, and is listed as one of the handful of authors on the <i>Awakening </i>core rulebook, alongside Bill Bridges. Yes, it's a different game with a different mythos and nominally different metaphysics, but nevertheless there's something to be said for the fact that of all the games that were re-imagined, Mage and its magic system changed the least, by an incomparable degree frankly. What I'm about to say is highly speculative, but I would not be surprised to find out that when they were cobbling together the first edition of Mage (on which Bridges was one of the original designers), that they sort of kicked themselves by having already used <i>Gnosis </i>for Werewolf (Bridges again), and went with the next closest thing.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Dataweaver</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1379141-how-to-fit-primers-and-mage-training-programs-into-the-game?p=1381951#post1381951\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">All of these are valid takes in what Arete could be, and none of them should be set up on a pedestal as being more essential than the others.</div> </div> </div> </div> My further textual argument for the validity of the Intelligence 4 requirement is this: to the extent that there are in fact multiple interpretations of what &quot;Arete&quot; and being &quot;Awake&quot; mean in the context of the game, and further, that some of those interpretations render parts of the game (including the materials in various supplements) contradictory, while others interpretations make them compatible--we should interpret the text (i.e., the game <i>writ </i>large) so that it is not at odds with itself, and we can give effect to as many of its rules/provisions as possible. I can't and won't take the position that I think you're definitely wrong, because that would intellectually dishonest; my interpretation could be wrong. But I will say that my model for how all of this works in the game assumes that the Intelligence 4 requirement was deliberated upon and given some due consideration by an individual with plenty of experience with the game line, and works from there to find a way of reading that requirement together with prior writings and rules, to formulate the most internally harmonious interpretation of the game as possible. In other words, if read a rule that doesn't jibe with my already formed notion of how the game <i>is</i>, I'm more inclined to change my notion to include and hold as true the new rule I read, than I am to excise the rule entirely. Mind you, I try to change my theory of the game in the most narrow way possible that allows it to work with whatever new rule I found, but my goal is still to make it work before I assume they made an outright mistake.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Sig","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2319-captotter","name":"CaptOtter","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2319_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"405"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-04-16T18:53:16-07:00","id":1382029,"title":"","content":"A few points: first, I fully agree with your philosophy as outlined in your last paragraph. I tend to do the same thing. That said, you have to keep in mind that Mage is built on at least two not entirely aligned visions: there's the 1e*/2e/SC/M20 vision of Phil Brucato, and there's the MRev visions of Jess Heinig (Guide to the Technocracy through Storyteller's Handbook), Bill Bridges (Storyteller's Handbook through Ascension), and Ryan Macklin (the revised Convention Books that came out a year before M20 was announced). A big part of the reason why M20 needed to take steps to heal a broken fanbase was because MRev broke the fanbase. Which is not to pass judgment on anyone; just to illustrate the distinctions that exist. So when MRev says one thing and M20 says something different, you need to ask why, and then decide whether you'll go with what MRev says, what M20 says, or some alternative of your own. <br /> <br /> MRev says that Primers require the reader to have Intelligence 4; M20 doesn't. Did M20 just forget to include that requirement, or was its absence deliberate? If it was deliberate, was it because Brucato didn't understand what Bridges was going for, or was it because Brucato <i>disagreed with</i> what Bridges did? And if Brucato disagreed with Bridges, was he right? <br /> <br /> In my view, the change was deliberate; it was done because Brucato saw the MRev version as needlessly burdonsome; and he was right. <br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:gray\">* I include 1e provisionally, as Brucato didn't actually get involved with Mage until after the core book was written; as such, there are a number of things in 1e core, and a handful in the earliest supplements, that don't mesh with Brucato's take on what Mage is. But such is what happens in the earliest days of a gameline.</span><br /> <br /> <hr /><br /> With regard to Ascension's Arete vs. Awakening's Gnosis: the Mage Translation Guide (which was comparing MRev to the first edition of Awakening) points or that the conceptual difference between the two is that Arete is about excellence and virtue while Gnosis is about uncovering arcane secrets. It's pointed out elsewhere in the Translation Guide that the whole of the metaphysics for Awakening align better with the beliefs of the Order of Hermes than with any other group in Ascension; in effect, Awakening is an example of what Ascension might have ended up looking like if the Order of Hermes had gotten its way back when the Council of Nine was founded, and the other proto-Traditions had been absorbed as Houses. So the fact that Awakening's Gnosis is conceptually similar to what you'd expect of the Hermetic notion of excellence and virtue (investigating arcane mysteries and building up a body of arcane lore) doesn't surprise me. <br /> <br /> I would add that a strong case could be made that Awakening represents what the MRev crew wanted Ascension to be; so it does provide insights into the design decisions made during the MRev era. But as I said at the start of this post, the vision(s) of MRev aren't quite the same as the vision behind every other edition of Mage. <br /> <br /> <hr /><br /> Finally: concerning Foundations vs. Arete, <i>my</i> argument is that the genius of what the Traditions came up with in Arete was that they divorced matters of Focus from Arete. That's the main difference between Arete and a Foundation: a Foundation is deeply tied to one specific view of what Magick is, while Arete tries to be agnostic on the subject by sticking to generalities: Arete is about excellence and virtue. What does that mean, precisely? That depends on which Tradition you ask (or in M20, which mage). The reason why the Traditions were able to buy into Arete was because the Verbena could still say that excellence and virtue is Spontaneity and sympathy with life's pulse while the Choristers could still say that excellence and virtue is Divinity and a relationship to the divine, etc.: they didn't have to compromise on the essentials, they just had to accept that there was a broader principle that as different and often contradictory as their beliefs seemed to be, they were all in some abstract sense doing the same thing.<br /> <br /> But again, that only worked because Arete <i>doesn't</i> insist on defining exactly what excellence and virtue is, certainly not to the extent of requiring it to prominently feature the specific kind of understanding that Intelligence represents.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div style=\"margin-left:40px\"> <div align=\"center\">My hacks: <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwtrin\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#0000FF\">Trinity Continuum</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwscion\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#008000\">Scion</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwExalt\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#DAA520\">Exalted</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwcwod\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#800000\">World of Darkness</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwnwod\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#FF0000\">Chronicles of Darkness</span></a></div> </div>","edited":{"userid":421,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/421-dataweaver","user":"Dataweaver","date":". 04-18-2020, 03:03 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/421-dataweaver","name":"Dataweaver","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar421_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"8420"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-04-25T11:16:43-07:00","id":1383803,"title":"","content":"I thought this discussion would be how to better integrate the use of Grimoires into the game world and how they're used - to lower XP. Both FbDF and M20 leave something to be desired with the implementation. A high enough level grimoire means the person learns Spheres for next to no XP, depending on interpretation.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2783-tytalus","name":"Tytalus","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2783_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"491"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}